          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "The boundary meets infinitely many chambers, (4)(ii). Keep $f$ and $I$ from step 2.2. For every $u\\in W_I$ the chamber $uC$ contains $f$, because $u\\in W_{S(f)}$ fixes $f$; distinct $u$ give distinct chambers: if $uC=u'C$, then with $L:=u'^{-1}u$ one has $L(C)=C$, and for any point $x\\in C^\\circ$, which is nonempty by [F21] and lies in $C$, the points $x$ and $L(x)$ are in $C$ and $L\\cdot x=L(x)$, so the collision theorem gives $L\\in W_{S(x)}=W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$ and $u=u'$. Since $W_I$ is infinite, the infinitely many distinct chambers $uC$ all contain $f$, so every neighborhood of $f$ meets infinitely many distinct chambers. Together with step 2.2 this is (4).",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
