        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Compact subsets. Let $K\\subseteq U^\\circ$ be compact. For each $x\\in K$ step 5.1 supplies a radius $\\varepsilon_x>0$ such that the ball about $x$ meets only finitely many chambers and only finitely many walls. These balls cover $K$, so compactness gives finitely many points $x_1,\\dots,x_m$ with $K\\subseteq\\bigcup_i\\{g:d(x_i,g)<\\varepsilon_{x_i}\\}$; a set that is contained in a finite union of sets, each of which is met by only finitely many chambers and walls, is itself met by only finitely many chambers and walls. Since every point of $U^\\circ$ has such a ball, every point of $U^\\circ$ has a neighborhood meeting only finitely many chambers and only finitely many walls.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F18",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-2",
          "claim": "Clause (2) and the vertex (5). First, $U^\\circ$ is $W$-invariant: for each $w$, the map $L_w(x)=w\\cdot x$ is a linear bijection by [F20], and as in step 5.1 its two coordinate matrices give $d(L_wx,L_wy)\\le C_wd(x,y)$ and $d(L_w^{-1}x,L_w^{-1}y)\\le C_w'd(x,y)$ by [F16], so $L_w$ is a homeomorphism; since $L_w(U)=U$ by [F1], the image $L_w(U^\\circ)$ is open and contained in $U$, hence $L_w(U^\\circ)\\subseteq U^\\circ$, and applying the same to $L_w^{-1}$ gives $L_w(U^\\circ)=U^\\circ$. Now if $f\\in U^\\circ$ then $f=w\\cdot g$ with $g\\in C$, and $g\\in U^\\circ$ by this invariance, so the criterion (1), whose two directions are steps 2.2 and 3.1, makes the group $W_{S(g)}$ finite and $g\\in C^f$, whence $f\\in w\\cdot C^f$; conversely every point of $w\\cdot C^f$ lies in $U^\\circ$ by the finite case of (1) applied to its $w$-translate in $C$ and the $W$-invariance of $U^\\circ$. Since $C^f=\\bigcup\\{C_I:I\\subseteq S,\\ W_I\\ \\text{finite}\\}$ and $f\\in C_I$ means $S(f)=I$, this is the identification $C^f=\\{f\\in C:W_{S(f)}\\ \\text{finite}\\}$ and hence $U^\\circ=\\bigcup_{w\\in W}w\\cdot C^f$. Applying (1) to $f=0$, whose zero set is $S(0)=S$, gives $0\\in U^\\circ$ if and only if $W_S=W$ is finite; this is (5).",
